# 909 Kick — BJT astable multivibrator (EXPERIMENTAL ENGINE PROBE)
#
# This pedal is a PROBE to determine whether the WDF compiler can handle
# a BJT astable multivibrator topology. It is NOT a production voice.
#
# Topology: classic symmetric NPN astable multivibrator.
#   - Q1, Q2: NPN transistors (2N3904 proxy for 2SC1583)
#   - R_c1, R_c2: collector pull-up resistors (10kΩ)
#   - R_b1, R_b2: base timing resistors (100kΩ)
#   - C_t1: Q1.collector → Q2.base timing cap (10nF)
#   - C_t2: Q2.collector → Q1.base timing cap (10nF)
#   - f ≈ 1/(1.4 × R_b × C_t) = 1/(1.4 × 100k × 10n) ≈ 714 Hz
#     (using standard astable formula; target ~100 Hz would need R_b ≈ 714kΩ
#      or C_t ≈ 71nF; we use a compact probe topology here)
#
# WHY 714 Hz not 100 Hz: the 909 BD oscillates at ~50-190 Hz but requires
# a large-valued RC timing network. For the engine probe we use the compact
# values (100kΩ, 10nF) to minimize solver complexity and still test the
# cross-coupled BJT topology. If this compiles, tuning to 100 Hz is trivial.
#
# Output tapped from Q1.collector via AC coupling capacitor.
#
# ENGINE GAP HYPOTHESIS: the WDF compiler handles BJTs in static amplifier
# topologies. The astable multivibrator has no stable DC operating point —
# both BJTs oscillate between saturated and cut-off states. The multi-NL
# grouped solver (BjtTwoPort) may find a DC fixed point at zero and fail
# to oscillate. If this test compiles but produces no AC output, it confirms
# the "DC fixed point capture" gap documented in ROADMAP M4.
pedal "909 BD BJT Probe" {
  supply 9V
  components {
    Q1: npn(2n3904)
    Q2: npn(2n3904)
    R_c1: resistor(10k)
    R_c2: resistor(10k)
    R_b1: resistor(100k)
    R_b2: resistor(100k)
    C_t1: cap(10n)
    C_t2: cap(10n)
    C_out: cap(100n)
    R_out: resistor(100k)
  }
  nets {
    # Collector pull-ups from supply
    vcc -> R_c1.a
    R_c1.b -> Q1.collector, C_t1.a

    vcc -> R_c2.a
    R_c2.b -> Q2.collector, C_t2.a

    # Cross-coupling: Q1.collector → C_t1 → Q2.base → R_b2 → gnd
    C_t1.b -> Q2.base, R_b2.a
    R_b2.b -> gnd

    # Cross-coupling: Q2.collector → C_t2 → Q1.base → R_b1 → gnd
    C_t2.b -> Q1.base, R_b1.a
    R_b1.b -> gnd

    # Emitters to ground
    Q1.emitter -> gnd
    Q2.emitter -> gnd

    # AC-coupled output from Q1.collector
    Q1.collector -> C_out.a
    C_out.b -> R_out.a
    R_out.b -> gnd
    C_out.b -> out

    # Trigger input: inject pulse into Q1.base to break symmetry
    in -> Q1.base
  }
  init {
    Q1: saturated
    Q2: cutoff
  }
}
